INT. ROADSIDE WAX MUSEUM - HALL OF PRESIDENTS - NIGHT

Dim corridor. Red velvet ropes. Wax figures stand on low platforms in dusty suits, their glass eyes catching the flashlight beams. A faded banner: "ONE DAY ONLY - FOREVER."

HARLAN BOONE (40s, museum owner, ring of keys, bathrobe over pajamas) leads four visitors down the hall, lantern swinging. Behind him: DOLORES KIM (50s, schoolteacher, tote bag), KIT ABERNATHY (20s, backpacker, headlamp), MASON TREVELYAN (30s, salesman, loosened tie), and YVETTE DUBOIS (30s, nurse, scrubs under a parka).

HARLAN
Right this way, folks. The museum closes at six, but I make exceptions for storms.

DOLORES
Thank you, Mr. Boone. We were nearly in a ditch.

HARLAN
Best place to be nearly in a ditch is my front door.

KIT
Are these all real people?

HARLAN
Wax, miss. All wax.

MASON
They look awfully real.

HARLAN
That's the craft.

Yvette stops at a figure of Abraham Lincoln. She reaches out, touches its sleeve, and pulls back.

YVETTE
It's warm.

HARLAN
That's the lamp.

YVETTE
There's no lamp.

Harlan keeps walking. The visitors exchange glances.

KIT
Is anyone else getting a weird vibe?

DOLORES
^Is that wax supposed to be breathing?

MASON
^Is that wax supposed to be breathing?

Dolores and Mason speak at once, then look at each other in alarm. All four turn toward the Lincoln figure.

Its head swivels slowly toward them.

YVETTE
Oh no.

Harlan stops at the end of the hall. Turns. His smile has changed. It is wider and wrong.

HARLAN
Don't be rude, folks. They've been waiting a very long time for company.

All along the hall, the wax figures stir. A hand lifts. A jaw clicks. A glass eye rolls forward.
